     I tried using both g-<move> and ctrl-move (by disabling the number_pad
option) and both of them go right past side-paths.
     I might suggest to the devlopment team that they take a look at Moria
v5.0 (or even v4.87) handles its shift-movement routine-- on Moria I use
it all the time.  It stops at side paths.
     Another feature I might suggest porting from Moria is infra-vision--
some classes (elf, perhaps, though ironically elves don't get IV in Moria
for some reason) would be able to see monsters two steps away, i.e.
instead of:        ##@
                   ^ bloodthirsty Yeenoghu or whatever
this type of character (might also be an aquired ability) would see:
                   &#@ (better in color, no doubt).
However, he wouldn't see
                   &##@ most likely.
This would keep characters from getting attacked first by monsters on the
same or lower speed level.
     Naturally, this is a reasonably valuble attribute, so it would have
to be difficult to acquire and, if granted to a class, not to a class that's
already especially powerful (a tourist or healer might start out wearing
"a pair of glasses" [a "monocle" for a healer would be perfect :) ] that
would grant such an ability, since they're rather weak as is.  Naturally,
these are subject to cursing (which might cause one to go blind), and
destruction via cursed enchant weapon scrolls and destroy armor scrolls
[below -1 would result in ineffectiveness].  A +3 monocle sounds rediculous,
but then again so is a +3 Hawaian shirt-- perhaps one can only enchant them
to +1.)

:The floating eye attack is particularly nasty (but the dungeon ain't no
:picnic, either). Singling out the floating eye stills seems like an
:idiosyncratic case to me. What about mimics? What about an option to
:confirm all attacks (in the sense you mention, that is, once you say yes
:then you can proceed to attack without question)? However to my way of
:thinking this would have to be an all or nothing deal, _i_ can't justify
:singling out the nasty eye.
     Not mimics, no: 1) it gives the players a strategic advantage,
2) one can already avoid mimics by leaving the room, noting which objects
disappear [can't see a parallel in reality to this], and then avoiding
them.  Overlay reads don't case problems here because you're inside a
room in most cases.
     An option to confirm all attacks would be a pain.  How about an
"attack" command?  One could have an "auto-attack" option, whereby
running into any monster attacks it.  Then a "semi-auto-attack" level
where any previously-attacked monster (via the attack command) is
attacked by running into it (not very intuitive, but good enough as
far as strategy goes; I'd probably use it) and then a "manual-attack"
level where you use the attack key in all cases (more consistant than
"semi-auto-attack").  It'd have to be a reachable key; the "-" key wouldn't
be a bad choice, or the "*" key.
     On the semi-auto level, running into monsters would give you a message
(what would be an appropriate message escapes me), not pass the turn, but
not give you a --more-- prompt if you run into it again (that doesn't sound
easy to implement, though).  On the manual level, it wouldn't even give you
a message, but act like the monster was a wall.
     Default would have to be auto-attack, since that's what everone's
(everyone)'s used to.
     Naturally, *I* would be satisfied with just an option to confirm
the first attack on floating eyes, even if it does sound trivial.  That's
got to be the single-most deadly move you can make that 1) you can't
protect yourself against without blinding yourself [a disadvantage],
2) you don't know about previously (in a hallway, for instance).
